# Instrumental variables and the LATE theorem
subtitle: "Why the gap between Oregon's lottery winners and losers is not the effect of Medicaid, and how dividing by the first stage recovers it for the compliers."
tags: ["instrumental variables","late","compliers","medicaid"]
teaches: ["methods/instrumental-variables","methods/late","concepts/compliance-types"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow"}
next: {"title":"Bad instruments, weak instruments","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/bad-instruments-weak-instruments.cast"}

## Winning is not coverage
voice: female
level: advanced
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Drawn
      - Not drawn
    values:
      - 40
      - 14
    colors:
      - '#4f8a4b'
      - '#8f887c'
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 0
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 100
    y_label: On Medicaid (%)
    label_size: 32
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 110
      w: 560
      h: 420
sources:
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein, Taubman, Wright, Bernstein, Gruber, Newhouse, Allen, Baicker and the Oregon Health Study Group (Quarterly Journal of Economics)
      year: 2012
      finding: 89,824 people signed up for a waiting list for about 10,000 Medicaid (OHP Standard) places; the state drew 35,169 people (29,664 households) by lottery in 2008. About 30% of those drawn enrolled in OHP Standard; being drawn raised the share ever on Medicaid during the study period by 25.6 percentage points (control mean 14.1%).

    text n_places "10,000 places" cites ["finkelstein2012"] x 800 y 600 font_size 40 hidden true
    text n_list "90,000 signed up" cites ["finkelstein2012"] x 800 y 545 font_size 40 hidden true
    text n_drawn "30,000 drawn" #4f8a4b cites ["finkelstein2012"] x 800 y 490 font_size 40 hidden true
    math z_eq tex "Z_i = 1" size 44 colors {"Z_i = 1":"#4f8a4b"} x 800 y 425 hidden true
    text fstage_title "First stage" #8f887c x 800 y 630 font_size 34 hidden true
    text fs "+26 points" #4f8a4b cites ["finkelstein2012"] x 800 y 570 font_size 52 hidden true
    text naive1 "Enrolled vs not" x 800 y 490 font_size 38 hidden true
    mark naive1_x around naive1 kind strike hidden true
    text naive2 "Drawn vs not drawn" #4f8a4b x 800 y 490 font_size 38 hidden true
    text sp_note "6-month spending, made up" #8f887c x 800 y 425 font_size 30 hidden true
    text sp_d "Drawn  $155" #4f8a4b x 800 y 365 font_size 42 hidden true
    text sp_n "Not drawn  $233" #8f887c x 800 y 305 font_size 42 hidden true

    card title "Winning is not coverage"

If a lottery hands out Medicaid at random, why isn't the gap between its winners and losers simply the effect of Medicaid?
    draw axes

Oregon tried exactly that in 2008. It had money fo (@draw n_places@)r about ten thousand more adults on Medicaid, and almost ninety thousand pe (@draw n_list@)ople put their names on the waiting list.

So the state drew about thirty thousand households at random. Call being drawn Z equals (@draw z_eq ends@) one.
    draw n_drawn

But a win was only a ticket: a winner still had to send back forms and show an income below the poverty line.
    highlight n_drawn

    erase n_places n_list n_drawn z_eq

    ask "What share of the people drawn were on Medicaid within a year or so? Set the bar to your guess." intro "First, a guess." on bar_1 store g default 70
        right: Close: you said {g:0} percent, and it was about {g.true:0}.
        wrong: You said {g:0} percent; it was about {g.true:0}.

About four in ten. Many forms never came back, and about half of those that did failed the income test.
    highlight bar_1

Among those not drawn, about fourteen in a hundred got onto Medicaid anyway, by other routes.
    draw bar_2

So winning raised coverage by about twenty-six percentage points. That jump is the lottery's first stage.
    draw fstage_title fs

The tempting next step is to compare the winners w (@highlight bar_1@)ho enrolled with the winners who didn't.
    draw naive1

But enrolling was a choice, (@draw naive1_x@) and whatever drove it, being sick, being organised, needing care, travels straight into their medical bills.

    erase naive1 naive1_x

The honest comparison keeps the lottery's own two groups (@highlight bar_1 bar_2@): everyone drawn against everyone not drawn, whatever they did next.
    draw naive2

To keep the arithmetic in view, take made-up numbers: over six months, the drawn spend  (@draw sp_d@)a hundred and fifty-five dollars out of pocket, the rest two (@draw sp_n@) hundred and thirty-three.
    draw sp_note

So winning c (@highlight sp_d sp_n@)ut spending by seventy-eight dollars a head. That gap is the intention-to-treat effect, and it is honest.
    text itt "Gap  −$78" #8a5fa8 x 800 y 235 font_size 50

But it is spread over everyone drawn, when the draw changed cove (@highlight fs@)rage for only twenty-six in every hundred.
    highlight itt

So who are those twenty-six, and what did coverage do for them? We'll sort the applicants into types, count the ones who moved, and divide.
    highlight itt fs effect circle

## Four kinds of applicant
voice: female
level: advanced
sources:
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein, Taubman, Wright, Bernstein, Gruber, Newhouse, Allen, Baicker and the Oregon Health Study Group (Quarterly Journal of Economics)
      year: 2012
      finding: 'Ever on Medicaid during the study period: 14.1% of those not drawn; being drawn raised it by 25.6 percentage points.'
    - id: air1996
      title: Identification of Causal Effects Using Instrumental Variables
      authors: Angrist, Imbens and Rubin (Journal of the American Statistical Association)
      year: 1996
      finding: Sorts units by their potential treatments into compliers, always-takers, never-takers and defiers, and shows the instrumental-variables ratio is the average effect for compliers under exclusion and monotonicity.

    text col_title "If not drawn" #8f887c x 700 y 730 font_size 46 hidden true
    math col_math tex D_i(0) size 50 x 960 y 730 hidden true
    text col_on "On" #2f6b8f x 520 y 660 font_size 46 hidden true
    text col_off "Off" #b5482e x 900 y 660 font_size 46 hidden true
    text row_title "If drawn" #8f887c x 120 y 470 font_size 46 hidden true
    math row_math tex D_i(1) size 50 x 120 y 400 hidden true
    text row_on "On" #2f6b8f x 270 y 505 font_size 46 hidden true
    text row_off "Off" #b5482e x 270 y 265 font_size 46 hidden true
    path grid_out thick #3d3833 points [[330,620],[1090,620],[1090,140],[330,140],[330,620]] hidden true
    path grid_v thick #3d3833 points [[710,620],[710,140]] hidden true
    path grid_h thick #3d3833 points [[330,380],[1090,380]] hidden true
    text at_name "Always-takers" #3d3833 x 520 y 540 font_size 50 hidden true
    text at_n "14" #3d3833 cites ["finkelstein2012"] x 520 y 450 font_size 80 hidden true
    text c_name "Compliers" #4f8a4b x 900 y 540 font_size 54 hidden true
    text c_n "26" #4f8a4b cites ["finkelstein2012"] x 900 y 450 font_size 80 hidden true
    text def_name "Defiers" #8f887c x 520 y 300 font_size 54 hidden true
    text def_n "0" #8f887c x 520 y 210 font_size 80 hidden true
    text nt_name "Never-takers" #3d3833 x 900 y 300 font_size 50 hidden true
    text nt_n "60" #3d3833 x 900 y 210 font_size 80 hidden true
    mark def_x around def_name kind strike hidden true
    math eq cites ["finkelstein2012"] tex "0.40 - 0.14 = {0.26}" size 60 colors {"{0.26}":"#4f8a4b"} x 710 y 65 hidden true
    text ben "Ben" x 130 y 215 font_size 58 hidden true
    text ben_q "?" #8f887c x 130 y 135 font_size 90 hidden true
    mark c_ring around c_name c_n kind circle hidden true
    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["col_title","col_math","col_on","col_off","row_title","row_math","row_on","row_off","grid_out","grid_v","grid_h","at_name","at_n","c_name","c_n","def_name","def_n","nt_name","nt_n","def_x","eq","ben","ben_q","c_ring"] hidden true

    card title "Four kinds of applicant"

Call being on Medicaid D. Before a single name is drawn, every applicant already has two answers: her D if drawn, and her D if not.
    draw row_title row_math col_title col_math

Each answer is yes or no, so the two answers sort the applicants into four kinds.
    draw grid_out grid_v grid_h col_on col_off row_on row_off

Some are on Medicaid either wa (@highlight row_on col_on@)y. These always-takers qualify by another route, such as a disability.
    draw at_name

Some stay off either way. Nev (@highlight row_off col_off@)er-takers never send the forms back, or turn out to earn too much.
    draw nt_name

And some are on Medicaid only i (@highlight row_on col_off@)f drawn. These compliers are the only people the lottery actually moves.
    draw c_name

The last cell would hold defiers, who'd sign up only if they lost. The method assumes thi (@draw def_x@)s cell is empty, and that assumption is called monotonicity.
    draw def_name

Here it is easy to believe: losing a lot (@highlight def_n@)tery gives nobody a new reason to enrol.
    draw def_n

Now count them. Because the draw was random, the drawn and the not drawn hold the same mix of the three kinds.
    highlight at_name c_name nt_name effect box

Among those not drawn, the ones on Medicaid sit in the left column, and with no defiers that means only always-takers: f (@draw at_n ends@)ourteen in a hundred.
    highlight col_on at_name def_n effect box

Among those drawn, the ones on Medicaid sit in the top row: always-takers and compliers together, forty in a hundred.
    highlight row_on at_name c_name effect box

Subtract, and the always-takers cancel. The first stage, twenty-six (@draw c_n@) points, is exactly the share of compliers, and the other s (@draw nt_n@)ixty are never-takers.
    draw eq

    quiz "Ana was not drawn, yet she is on Medicaid. Which kind of applicant is she?" intro "Try one."
        right: An always-taker: she is covered without a win, and with defiers ruled out, only always-takers are.
        wrong: Look at the left column: who is on Medicaid without being drawn?
        * A complier
        + An always-taker
        * A never-taker

Now take Ben. He was drawn, and he is on Medicaid. Is he a complier?
    draw ben

He could be either kind i (@highlight at_name c_name effect box@)n the top row. Only his coverage had he lost would tell us, and that is the future nobody ever sees.
    draw ben_q

So compliers can be counted to the percentage point, but not one of them can ever be named.
    draw c_ring

    erase ben ben_q

Angrist, Imbens and Rubin set out these four kinds in 1996. What's left is to measure the compliers' effect without ever finding them.
    highlight at_name c_name def_name nt_name effect box

## Divide by the people who moved
voice: female
level: advanced
vars:
    s:
      value: 0.26
      min: 0.05
      max: 0.6
      step: 0.01
    itt:
      expr: '-300*s'
    late:
      expr: itt/s
    err:
      expr: 10/s
sources:
    - id: imbens1994
      title: Identification and Estimation of Local Average Treatment Effects
      authors: Imbens and Angrist (Econometrica)
      year: 1994
      finding: 'Under independence, exclusion, a non-zero first stage and monotonicity, the instrumental-variables (Wald) ratio equals the average treatment effect for compliers: the local average treatment effect.'

    text h_share "Share" #8f887c x 395 y 690 font_size 42 hidden true
    text h_d "If drawn" #4f8a4b x 620 y 690 font_size 42 hidden true
    text h_n "If not drawn" #8f887c x 860 y 690 font_size 42 hidden true
    text h_eff "Effect" #8a5fa8 x 1060 y 690 font_size 42 hidden true
    path rule_h thick #3d3833 points [[40,650],[1110,650]] hidden true
    text at_name "Always-takers" #3d3833 x 150 y 585 font_size 44 hidden true
    text at_share "14" x 395 y 585 font_size 54 hidden true
    text at_d "$400" #2f6b8f x 620 y 585 font_size 56 hidden true
    text at_n "$400" #2f6b8f x 860 y 585 font_size 56 hidden true
    text at_eff "0" #8a5fa8 x 1060 y 585 font_size 56 hidden true
    text c_name "Compliers" #4f8a4b x 150 y 495 font_size 44 hidden true
    text c_share "26" #4f8a4b x 395 y 495 font_size 54 hidden true
    text c_d "$150" #2f6b8f x 620 y 495 font_size 56 hidden true
    text c_n "$450" #b5482e x 860 y 495 font_size 56 hidden true
    text c_eff "−300" #8a5fa8 x 1060 y 495 font_size 56 hidden true
    text nt_name "Never-takers" #3d3833 x 150 y 405 font_size 44 hidden true
    text nt_share "60" x 395 y 405 font_size 54 hidden true
    text nt_d "$100" #b5482e x 620 y 405 font_size 56 hidden true
    text nt_n "$100" #b5482e x 860 y 405 font_size 56 hidden true
    text nt_eff "0" #8a5fa8 x 1060 y 405 font_size 56 hidden true
    path rule_a #8f887c points [[40,355],[1110,355]] style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    text avg_name "Average" x 150 y 300 font_size 44 hidden true
    text avg_d "$155" #4f8a4b x 620 y 300 font_size 56 hidden true
    text avg_n "$233" #8f887c x 860 y 300 font_size 56 hidden true
    text avg_gap "−78" #8a5fa8 x 1060 y 300 font_size 60 hidden true
    math wald tex "\\frac{E[Y \\mid Z=1]-E[Y \\mid Z=0]}{E[D \\mid Z=1]-E[D \\mid Z=0]}" size 46 x 560 y 150 hidden true
    math live tex "\\frac{{itt}}{{s}} = {late}" size 56 x 470 y 150 hidden true
    math noise tex "\\pm 10 \\;\\to\\; \\pm {err:0}" size 46 x 900 y 150 hidden true
    text tag_mon "Monotonicity" #3d3833 x 150 y 215 font_size 40 hidden true
    text tag_rel "Relevance" #3d3833 x 395 y 215 font_size 40 hidden true
    text tag_ind "Independence" #3d3833 x 740 y 215 font_size 40 hidden true
    text tag_exc "Exclusion" #3d3833 x 1060 y 215 font_size 40 hidden true
    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["h_share","h_d","h_n","h_eff","rule_h","at_name","at_share","at_d","at_n","at_eff","c_name","c_share","c_d","c_n","c_eff","nt_name","nt_share","nt_d","nt_n","nt_eff","rule_a","avg_name","avg_d","avg_n","avg_gap","wald","live","noise","tag_mon","tag_rel","tag_ind","tag_exc"] hidden true

    card title "Divide by the people who moved"

Now the money. Call spending out of pocket over six months Y: here is what each kind of applicant spends, drawn or not, in the same made-up numbers.
    draw h_share h_d h_n h_eff rule_h

Always-takers are covered either way, so the draw changes nothing for them: four hundred dollars, drawn or not.
    draw at_name at_share at_d at_n

Never-takers are uncovered either way: a hundred dollars, drawn or not.
    draw nt_name nt_share nt_d nt_n

So winning does nothing for either group, and those zeros are an assumption with a nam (@highlight h_eff@)e, exclusion: the letter may change spending only by changing coverage.
    draw at_eff nt_eff

In Oregon it rules out, say, a winner who never enrols but stops worrying about bills, or books a check-up because the letter came.
    highlight at_eff nt_eff effect circle

Compliers are covered only if drawn: a hundred and fifty dollars then, four hundred and fifty if not.
    draw c_name c_share c_d c_n

So co (@highlight c_d c_n@)verage saves each complier three hundred dollars, though no data set will ever show us this row.
    draw c_eff

What the data do show are the column averages across all hundred: a hundred and fifty-five dollars against tw (@draw avg_n@)o hundred and thirty-three.
    draw rule_a avg_name avg_d

Their gap, minus seventy-eight, comes entirely from the complie (@highlight c_share c_eff@)rs' row: twenty-six people saving three hundred each, spread across a hundred.
    draw avg_gap

So divide the gap by the share who moved, the reduced form over the first stage: minus sev (@morph wald tex "\\frac{-78}{0.26} = {-300}"@)enty-eight over zero point two six is minus three hundred, exactly the compliers' saving.
    draw wald

That ratio is the Wald estimator. In 1994, Imbens and Angrist showed what it measures under these assumptions: the average effect for compliers, the local average treatment effect.
    highlight wald part "{-300}" effect circle

    erase wald

Each of the four assumptions holds up one piece of this table. Relevance: the draw must move someone, so (@highlight c_share@) the compliers' share is not zero.
    draw tag_rel

Independence: the draw is unrelated to each person's potential coverage and potential spend (@highlight at_d at_n c_d c_n nt_d nt_n@)ing, so the shares and the dollars are the same drawn or not.
    draw tag_ind

Exclusion keeps those (@highlight at_eff nt_eff@) zeros at zero, and monotonicity keeps out  (@draw tag_mon@)a defier row that would pull the other way.
    draw tag_exc

    quiz "Suppose the letter itself got never-takers a free check-up, saving each of them $13. What would the Wald ratio now report?" intro "Now break one."
        right: Minus three hundred and thirty: the never-takers' savings add about eight dollars to the gap, and dividing by 0.26 hands all of it to the compliers.
        wrong: The never-takers' thirteen dollars now sit in the gap too: weight them by their sixty in a hundred, add, then divide.
        * −$300
        * −$270
        + −$330

That is what a broken exclusion looks like: the letter's own effect on never-takers is credited to compliers, and the estimate grows by a tenth.
    highlight nt_eff effect circle

    erase tag_rel tag_ind tag_exc tag_mon

Now set  (@draw live noise@)the table aside and picture a weaker lottery, one that moves fewer people.
    fade h_share h_d h_n h_eff rule_h at_name at_share at_d at_n at_eff c_name c_share c_d c_n c_eff nt_name nt_share nt_d nt_n nt_eff rule_a avg_name avg_d avg_n avg_gap to 0.15

Grab the share under the line and move it toward zero. Watch the gap above it, the answer, and the error on the right.
    explore params ["s"] watch "Watch the share fall toward zero: the gap shrinks, the answer stays, the error beside it grows."

As the share falls, the gap shrinks with it, and the ratio stays at minus three hundred.
    animate s 0.1 duration 4

But a ten-dollar error in the gap becomes a hundred-dollar error when only one in ten moved, and a small breach of exclusion, like those thirteen dollars, is blown up the same way.
    highlight noise effect circle

## What Oregon found, and for whom
voice: female
level: advanced
sources:
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein, Taubman, Wright, Bernstein, Gruber, Newhouse, Allen, Baicker and the Oregon Health Study Group (Quarterly Journal of Economics)
      year: 2012
      finding: 'First-year mail survey: being drawn raised the share ever on Medicaid by 29.0 percentage points (control 13.5%) and lowered the share with any out-of-pocket medical spending in the last six months by 5.8 points (control 55.5%); the instrumental-variables effect of coverage is −20.0 points (SE 2.6). Survey response rate about 50% (weighted). 2SLS with lottery-draw and household-size controls.'
    - id: baicker2013
      title: 'The Oregon Experiment: Effects of Medicaid on Clinical Outcomes'
      authors: Baicker, Taubman, Allen, Bernstein, Gruber, Newhouse, Schneider, Wright, Zaslavsky, Finkelstein and the Oregon Health Study Group (New England Journal of Medicine)
      year: 2013
      finding: 'About two years after the lottery, 12,229 people in the Portland area: Medicaid lowered positive depression screens by 9.15 percentage points (95% CI −16.70 to −1.60; control 30%); no significant effect on blood pressure, cholesterol or glycated haemoglobin (6.5% or more in 5.1% of controls).'
takeaways:
    - Winning's gap is a diluted effect
    - Divide by the first stage
    - The effect belongs to compliers

    text hA "Year one" #8f887c x 200 y 690 font_size 42 hidden true
    text h_base "Not drawn" #8f887c x 620 y 690 font_size 42 hidden true
    text h_gap "Winning" #4f8a4b x 920 y 690 font_size 42 hidden true
    path ruleA thick #3d3833 points [[40,650],[1090,650]] hidden true
    text r1_lab "On Medicaid" #3d3833 x 200 y 595 font_size 46 hidden true
    text r1_base "13.5%" cites ["finkelstein2012"] x 620 y 595 font_size 54 hidden true
    text r1_gap "+29 points" #4f8a4b cites ["finkelstein2012"] x 920 y 595 font_size 54 hidden true
    text r2_lab "Paid out of pocket" x 200 y 505 font_size 46 hidden true
