    text r2_gap "−5.8 points" cites ["finkelstein2012"] x 920 y 505 font_size 54 hidden true
    math wald cites ["finkelstein2012"] tex "\\frac{-5.8}{0.29} \\approx -20" size 54 x 560 y 395 hidden true
    text wald_lab "points, for compliers" #8a5fa8 x 920 y 395 font_size 40 hidden true
    path ruleB #8f887c points [[40,290],[1090,290]] style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    text hB "Two years on" #8f887c x 200 y 250 font_size 42 hidden true
    text dep_lab "Depression" x 200 y 180 font_size 46 hidden true
    text dep_v "−9 points" #8a5fa8 cites ["baicker2013"] x 640 y 180 font_size 54 hidden true
    text bs_lab "Blood sugar" x 200 y 100 font_size 46 hidden true
    text bs_v "no clear change" #8f887c cites ["baicker2013"] x 700 y 100 font_size 50 hidden true
    text wald_ci "95%: −25 to −15" #8f887c cites ["finkelstein2012"] x 920 y 340 font_size 42 hidden true
    text dep_ci "95%: −17 to −2" #8f887c cites ["baicker2013"] x 960 y 180 font_size 42 hidden true
    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["hA","h_base","h_gap","ruleA","r1_lab","r1_base","r1_gap","r2_lab","r2_base","r2_gap","wald","wald_lab","ruleB","hB","dep_lab","dep_v","bs_lab","bs_v","wald_ci","dep_ci"] hidden true

    card title "What Oregon found, and for whom"

Oregon's researchers ran essentially this division on real data, and their first-year mail survey shows it cleanly.
    draw hA h_base h_gap ruleA

Only about half of those surveyed answered. Among them, about thirteen in a hundred not drawn were on Medicaid,  (@draw r1_gap@)and winning added twenty-nine points.
    draw r1_lab r1_base

Then one question: did you pay anything out of  (@highlight r2_lab effect box@)pocket for medical care in the last six months?
    draw r2_lab

Among those not drawn, about fifty-six percent said yes. Among those drawn, (@draw r2_gap@) it was almost six points fewer: the reduced form.
    draw r2_base

Divide the one by the other, and coverage cut the share paying out of pocket by about  (@draw wald_lab@)twenty percentage points for compliers, give (@draw wald_ci@) or take five.
    draw wald

About two years in, a second study examined some twelve thousand people around  (@draw dep_lab@)Portland. Coverage cut positive depression sc (@draw dep_v@)reens by about nine points from a base of thirty, though anything fr (@draw dep_ci@)om two to seventeen fits the data.
    draw ruleB hB

But on blood pressure, cholesterol and blood sugar, it found no effect  (@draw bs_v@)it could tell apart from zero.
    draw bs_lab

Some read that as coverage doing little for physical health in two years. Others answer that only about one in twenty had high blood sugar to begin with, too few to show even a sizeable improvement.
    highlight bs_v

What would tell them apart is a larger sample of people with diabetes, or a longer follow-up.
    highlight bs_lab effect underline

Either way, every number here belongs to lottery compliers: the adults who would enrol only when drawn.
    highlight wald_lab dep_v

    quiz "A clinic mails flu-shot reminders at random. Vaccinated: 50% with a reminder, 30% without. Caught flu: 9% against 10%. What does the Wald ratio say?" intro "A new case."
        right: Minus one point over twenty points of vaccination: five points less flu, for the people a reminder gets vaccinated, not for those who'd go anyway or never.
        wrong: Divide the drop in flu by the rise in vaccination, then ask whose vaccination the letter changed.
        * Flu down 1 point, for everyone
        * Flu down 5 points, for everyone reminded
        + Flu down 5 points, for those the reminder moved

    fade hA h_base h_gap ruleA r1_lab r1_base r1_gap r2_lab r2_base r2_gap wald wald_lab ruleB hB dep_lab dep_v bs_lab bs_v wald_ci dep_ci to 0.07

So the g (@draw takeaway_1@)ap between Oregon's winners and losers was never the effect of Medicaid: it was that effect, carried by about a quarter of them and averaged over everyone.

Dividi (@draw takeaway_2@)ng by the first stage takes the dilution off, but only exclusion and monotonicity make that division honest.

And th (@draw takeaway_3@)e answer always belongs to someone: the people the instrument moved. Change the instrument, and you change who they are.

That is the bargain an instrument offers: a credible number, as long as you say whose it is.

